            <ArticleTitle>Comprehensive behavioral phenotyping of calpastatin-knockout mice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1186/1756-6606-1-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Calpastatin is an endogenous inhibitor of calpain, intracellular calcium-activated protease. It has been suggested to be involved in molecular mechanisms of long-term plasticity and excitotoxic pathways. However, functions of calpastatin in vivo are still largely unknown. To examine the physiological roles of calpastatin, we subjected calpastatin-knockout mice to a comprehensive behavioral test battery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Calpastatin-knockout mice showed decreased locomotor activity under stressful environments, and decreased acoustic startle response, but we observed no significant change in hippocampus-dependent memory function.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These results suggest that calpastatin is likely to be more closely associated with affective rather than cognitive aspects of brain function.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
